# Query 7: find every individual whose tech:Customer type came from
# owl:equivalentClass entailment (e.g. sap:Client, crm:Customer,
# svcnow:BusinessPartner individuals), not a directly-asserted
# "rdf:type tech:Customer" triple.
# Demonstrates: OPTIONAL plus FILTER to show every OTHER type an
# individual also carries. Run this on the raw graph (1 result: only
# the canonical inst:customer-northwind-logistics, directly asserted)
# and on the OWL-RL-closed graph (4 individuals, 4 inferred types each:
# Customer/BusinessPartner/Client/Thing, confirmed by actually
# running this against this milestone's data). The query text never
# changes; only the data it runs against does. See docs/ontology.md sections 10, 12, 13.
#
# A subtlety this exact query surfaced in practice, not just in theory:
# svcnow:organization_xyz (the ServiceNow-shaped record this milestone
# deliberately leaves UNLINKED in data/ontology/instances/canonical_entity_resolution.ttl,
# with no owl:sameAs or even tech:candidateSameAs to the canonical
# Northwind Logistics individual) STILL appears in this query's
# reasoned-mode results, typed tech:Customer, purely because its own
# asserted class (svcnow:BusinessPartner) is schema-mapped to
# tech:Customer, independent of whether its IDENTITY was ever
# resolved. "Is type-compatible with Customer" (true here) and "IS the
# same real-world customer as our canonical record" (still unconfirmed
# here) are two separate claims this model tracks separately. Treating
# the first as proof of the second would be exactly the unjustified
# automatic merge docs/ontology.md section 11 warns against. See
# scripts/ontology_demo.py --demo interop's printed output.
PREFIX tech: <http://techfusion.example/ontology/v1#>

SELECT ?customer ?viaOtherType
WHERE {
    ?customer a tech:Customer .
    OPTIONAL {
        ?customer a ?viaOtherType .
        FILTER(?viaOtherType != tech:Customer && ?viaOtherType != tech:EnterpriseEntity)
    }
}
LIMIT 50
